<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Mola Has Many Colors!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Respond creatively to stories read. reading2: Summarize simple stories or articles. phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond creatively by identifying colors and shapes in stories about molas (e.g., 'red, blue, round.'). Can summarize a short text about mola art, mentioning key ideas (e.g., 'Molas have bright colors.'). Can spell and read one-syllable or two-syllable words using phonemic awareness and alphabet knowledge related to colors and instructions (e.g., 'orange,' 'vibrant,' and 'square').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large, colorful pictures of different molas and asks students to name colors and shapes they see. (e.g., "What colors do you see? Red! Blue! Yellow!" "What shapes do you see? Round! Square!"). This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary (mola, colors, shapes) from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "mola" and points to a picture of a mola. The teacher pronounces /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors' clearly, and then points to different colors in a mola picture, naming them (e.g., "This mola has red. This mola has blue."). The teacher also models reading short phrases like "a red mola" or "many colors" to prepare for reading. The teacher models how to spell simple words like 'red' and 'blue' using alphabet knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key words related to molas, colors, and shapes.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is a mola an animal or an art piece?" (Expected answer: "Art piece.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Are molas usually just one color or many colors?" (Expected answer: "Many colors.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Beautiful Mola")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short descriptive text about "The Beautiful Mola" displayed on the board or as a handout. The text will describe a specific mola design (e.g., featuring a turtle or a jaguar) and its colors.
                    <br>Example Text: "This is a beautiful mola. It has many colors: red, blue, and golden. It has a big round turtle in the middle. The mola also has small square shapes around the turtle. I like this mola because it is very vibrant and unique. It shows Kuna culture and creativity."
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding simple stories or descriptions to identify key details (A1.2).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions & Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud clearly, pointing to the words. Students follow along in silence.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What animal is in the mola?" (Expected answer: "A turtle.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students say "True" or "False."
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The mola has only one color." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The turtle is round." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like this mola because it is vibrant." (True, based on the text example)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and circle target vocabulary (mola, creativity, culture, turtle, round, vibrant, unique, golden) and grammar ('has', 'because') in the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying key information about molas (colors, shapes, animals); using 'A mola has...' and 'because'; reading one-syllable and two-syllable words related to colors and instructions (e.g., 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with the descriptive text from Stage 2, but with key words (colors, animal names, adjectives like 'vibrant') missing. Students read the text again and fill in the blanks using a word bank. (e.g., "This mola has __________ colors: red, blue, and golden. It has a big round __________.") This reinforces vocabulary (vibrant, golden, turtle, unique) and encourages careful reading.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Matching/Sequencing):</b> The teacher gives students strips of paper, each with a simple sentence describing steps for a very basic "paper mola project" (e.g., "First, choose your colors." "Next, cut the shapes carefully." "Last, paste the pieces."). Students read the sentences and put them in the correct instructional sequence. This uses instructional steps (first, next, last) and verbs (cut, paste, choose, express) and adverbs (carefully).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Reading & Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students read their completed gap-fill sentences from Activity 1 and their sequenced instructions from Activity 2 to each other. They listen to their partner and check if the words and order are correct according to the original text/logical steps. They practice pronouncing /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors' and reading words like 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second.'</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Beautiful Mola")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Descriptive Sharing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will read short descriptions about different imaginary molas and then creatively respond by describing their favorite one using target language about colors and shapes. This connects to "The Beautiful Mola" and "A Mola Has Many Colors!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives 3-4 simple, short paragraphs describing different molas (e.g., "This mola has a jaguar. It is golden and black." "This mola has a butterfly. It is red, orange, and turquoise. It is very vibrant."). The texts incorporate vocabulary (jaguar, butterfly, turquoise, golden, vibrant, traditional, unique) and grammar ('A mola has...', 'because').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes a turn reading one of the mola descriptions aloud to their group. They are encouraged to read carefully and expressively.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading all the descriptions, each student chooses their favorite mola from the texts and writes one or two sentences expressing their preference, using 'I like this mola because...' (e.g., "I like this mola because it has many shapes." or "I like the jaguar mola because it is golden and unique.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then share their preference with their group, reading their sentences aloud. They also practice acknowledging peers' contributions (e.g., 'Your mola is very creative!' 'I like your color choices.').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group reading and sharing (Stage 4). The teacher circulates and observes students' ability to read, identify details, and use target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will read aloud their self-created sentence(s) describing their preferred mola (e.g., "I like this mola because it has a big red butterfly and round shapes."). The teacher will assess their reading fluency and content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (mola, colors, shapes, vibrant, unique) and sounds (/m/, /k/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words (e.g., 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of 'because' to express a reason for preference.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in identifying colors, shapes, or animals mentioned in the texts.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about molas and find colors like _______ and _______."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read words like 'vibrant' and 'second'." (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new things did you read about molas today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about reading the mola descriptions?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., engagement with pictures, gap-fill activity).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with reading comprehension or phonemic awareness (e.g., more direct phonetic practice, simpler texts, more visual aids)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the target grammar ('because,' present simple) and vocabulary (vibrant, unique, cut, paste, carefully) used naturally in the reading tasks?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and descriptive phrases learned today to talk about molas with their peers. They will be able to express preferences and discuss creative projects, building on their reading comprehension of mola characteristics.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>